Make User's requests a family: Request, Latent deriving it, the Editor dispatching by case

Dev accepted the Request family on 2026-10-13 ("I accept the Request family in User,
proceed"), the first design point of the Read request: the Editor is one actor with one
message type, so a second request needs a shape the requests share.

draco.dreams.user.Request is the abstract head of the family — no elements, no factory,
as ActorType is. Latent derives it (`type Latent from Request`); User holds Latent,
Request and Response. The Editor's message type is `draco dreams user Request` and its
message body is one branch, `case latent draco.dreams.user.Latent [ ... ]`, the whole
validation as before with `latent` where `msg` was — the corpus's first cross-package
case dispatch (the ash scenario's two branches are same-package). The generator writes
`case latent: draco.dreams.user.Latent =>` and its own `case _ => ()` catch-all, so a
Request with no branch is a no-op (drake.dlt CASE-BRANCH, effects shape). Read and
Delete join as further branches without touching the message type again. In Haskell:
Request is the sum type, the constructors its members, the Editor a function over it.

Two engine gaps found by that one branch, both closed in src/mods:
- Drake.parse read every lower-case-initial token of a case head as the binder, so the
  dotted type `draco.dreams.user.Latent` LOST the binder and was carried as the branch's
  NAME; the Scala rendered `case draco.dreams.user.Latent =>`, a value pattern on the
  companion. The branch type is a value-type slot, dotted outside the authoring package
  like every other: a token whose LAST segment is upper-case is now the type.
- DracoGenerator.externalImports scanned only an action's top-level statements, so the
  `[Problem]+` binding inside the branch lost `import scala.collection.mutable`. The
  scan now looks one level down into a case body.

